<section class="nc-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>U.S. kitchen measures in milliliters</h2>
    <p>U.S. kitchen units are built on doubling, which makes them easy to scale once you know the chain: 3 teaspoons make a tablespoon, 2 tablespoons make a fluid ounce, 16 tablespoons make a cup, and from there each step doubles: 2 cups to a pint, 2 pints to a quart, 4 quarts to a gallon.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>U.S. unit</th><th>Equals</th><th class="num">Milliliters</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1 teaspoon</td><td>1/3 tablespoon</td><td class="num">4.93</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 tablespoon</td><td>3 teaspoons</td><td class="num">14.79</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 fluid ounce</td><td>2 tablespoons</td><td class="num">29.57</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 cup</td><td>8 fluid ounces</td><td class="num">236.59</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 pint</td><td>2 cups</td><td class="num">473.18</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 quart</td><td>2 pints</td><td class="num">946.35</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 gallon</td><td>4 quarts, 16 cups</td><td class="num">3,785.41</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Not every "cup" is the same cup</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>U.S. customary cup:</strong> 236.59 mL. This is the cup in most American recipes and measuring sets.</li>
      <li><strong>U.S. nutrition-label cup:</strong> 240 mL. The FDA rounds the cup for Nutrition Facts serving sizes, so a label's "1 cup" is about 1.4% larger.</li>
      <li><strong>Metric cup:</strong> 250 mL, used in recipes from Australia, New Zealand, and Canada. It is about 5.7% larger than the U.S. cup, which matters in baking.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For dry ingredients, volume is the weak link. A cup of flour can weigh noticeably more when scooped than when spooned and leveled. If a recipe gives grams, use a scale.</p>

    <h2>U.S. vs. imperial gallons</h2>
    <p>The imperial gallon used in the UK is 4.546 L, about 20% larger than the U.S. gallon of 3.785 L. The pint follows suit: a UK pint is 20 imperial fluid ounces, a U.S. pint is 16 U.S. fluid ounces. This explains why British fuel-economy figures look better: a car rated 30 mpg in the U.S. would be about 36 mpg in UK terms, with no difference in the car.</p>

    <h2>Cubic feet, gallons, and cubic yards</h2>
    <p>1 cubic foot holds 7.48 U.S. gallons. To find how much water a tank, pond, or tub holds, multiply length × width × depth in feet, then by 7.48. A 4 × 2 × 2 ft tank is 16 cubic feet, or about 120 gallons. At roughly 8.34 lb per gallon, that water weighs about 1,000 lb, so check what the floor or stand can carry.</p>
    <p>Landscape materials work in cubic yards: 1 cubic yard is 27 cubic feet, or about 202 gallons. Mulch and soil bags are usually sold in cubic feet, so a 2 cu ft bag takes 13.5 bags to make a yard. Compare that with the bulk price per yard before buying bags for a large bed.</p>
  </div>
  <section class="hub-faq" aria-labelledby="guide-faq-title"><h2 id="guide-faq-title">Volume conversion questions</h2>
    <details><summary>How many cups are in a gallon?</summary><p>16 U.S. cups make 1 U.S. gallon. There are 4 cups in a quart and 2 in a pint.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How many milliliters are in a cup?</summary><p>A U.S. customary cup is 236.59 mL. Nutrition labels use 240 mL, and metric recipes use 250 mL.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How many liters are in a gallon?</summary><p>A U.S. gallon is 3.785 liters. An imperial (UK) gallon is 4.546 liters.</p></details>
  </section>
